// Cross-target fixture: `null`, and the values that may or may not be there.
//
// A function like the prelude's `stdlibFind` returns the element it found *or*
// nothing, so one machine word has to carry both -- and every backend has to
// agree with the evaluator on which one it is printing. That is what this
// fixture pins down: the word `null` for the empty case, the value itself
// otherwise, for each type a value can be.
//
// The found case allocates (the element is boxed to share the empty case's
// representation) while the list being searched is still live, so this also
// exercises the collector with a half-formed answer in a rooted slot.

println(null)
val nothing = null
println(nothing)

// Found and not found, over the same list.
println(stdlibFind([1, 2, 3, 4], (x) => x > 2))
println(stdlibFind([1, 2], (x) => x > 5))
println(stdlibFind([10, 20, 30], (x) => x > 15))

// Bound rather than printed straight away: the answer sits in a slot across
// the next call, which is where a missing root would show up.
val found = stdlibFind([10, 20], (x) => x > 15)
val missing = stdlibFind([10, 20], (x) => x > 99)
println(stdlibRange(0, 3))
println(found)
println(missing)

// The join itself, both ways round, for each type of value.
println(if (true) null else 7)
println(if (false) null else 7)
println(if (true) 7 else null)
println(if (false) 7 else null)
println(if (false) null else "s")
println(if (true) null else "s")
println(if (false) null else true)
println(if (false) null else false)
println(if (true) null else false)
println("done")
